WEBVTT

00:01:57.288 --> 00:02:00.038
whether an herbal ingredient 
on the market causes nausea.

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:02.371
Rather than deliberately 
giving people something

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:03.913
that might make them nauseated,

00:02:03.954 --> 00:02:06.829
we’d find those who already take
the ingredient every day.

00:02:06.871 --> 00:02:08.663
This group is called “the cohort”.

00:02:08.704 --> 00:02:11.246
We’d also need 
a comparison group of people

00:02:11.288 --> 00:02:13.371
who do not have exposure 
to the ingredient.

00:02:13.413 --> 00:02:15.329
And we would then compare statistics.

00:02:15.371 --> 00:02:18.288
If the rate of nausea 
is higher in the herbal cohort,

00:02:18.329 --> 00:02:22.079
it suggests an association
between the herbal supplement and nausea.

00:02:22.204 --> 00:02:24.329
Epidemiological studies are great tools

00:02:24.371 --> 00:02:26.996
to study the health effects 
of almost anything,

00:02:27.038 --> 00:02:29.246
without directly 
interfering in people’s lives

00:02:29.288 --> 00:02:31.829
or assigning them to potentially 
dangerous exposures.